    {
      "text": "I have the publication metadata and the summary. However, the excerpt tool did not return reliable results — it returned the same mismatched passage for both Chapter 1 and Chapter 19 (the text appears to be from a different work entirely), and I won't present fabricated or incorrect passages to you. Here is everything I can confirm accurately:\n\n---\n\n## 1. 📚 Publication History\n\n- **Original Publication Date:** July 11, 1960\n- **Original Publisher:** J.B. Lippincott & Co. (Philadelphia)\n- **ISBN (current standard edition):** 978-0-06-112008-4 (HarperCollins Perennial Modern Classics)\n\n**Notable Edition History:**\n- **1960** — First edition, J.B. Lippincott & Co. First printings are highly collectible.\n- **1962** — Paperback edition released alongside the Academy Award–winning film adaptation (dir. Robert Mulligan, starring Gregory Peck).\n- **1988** — Warner Books mass-market paperback became dominant in schools.\n- **2002** — HarperCollins Perennial Modern Classics edition, now the standard classroom text.\n- **2010** — 50th Anniversary Edition, HarperCollins, with a new foreword; widely used in academic settings.\n- **2015** — *Go Set a Watchman* (Lee's earlier manuscript/draft) published, reigniting scholarly debate about Scout/Atticus characterization.\n- The novel has been **translated into more than 40 languages** and has sold over **45 million copies** worldwide. It won the **Pulitzer Prize for Fiction in 1961**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. 🗺️ Major Plot Arcs & Themes\n\n**Plot Arcs:**\n\n| Arc | Description |\n|---|---|\n| **The Boo Radley Arc** | Scout, Jem, and Dill are fascinated by their reclusive neighbor Arthur \"Boo\" Radley. Over the course of the novel, childhood fear gradually transforms into understanding and empathy. |\n| **The Tom Robinson Trial** | Atticus Finch is appointed to defend Tom Robinson, a Black man falsely accused of raping Mayella Ewell. Despite clear evidence of innocence, Tom is convicted by an all-white jury — the novel's central indictment of systemic racism. |\n| **Scout's Coming-of-Age** | Narrated in retrospect by an adult Jean Louise (\"Scout\") reflecting on her childhood, the novel traces her moral education — learning to \"climb into someone's skin and walk around in it.\" |\n| **Bob Ewell's Revenge** | After the trial humiliates him, Bob Ewell attacks Scout and Jem. Boo Radley intervenes and saves the children, completing his transformation from monster to protector. |\n\n**Key Themes:**\n- ⚖️ **Racial Injustice** — The trial exposes the brutal mechanics of racism in the Jim Crow South.\n- 🦁 **Moral Courage** — Atticus models integrity in the face of community hostility.\n- 🧒 **Childhood & Innocence** — Scout's child-eye narration provides ironic distance from adult moral failures.\n- 🤝 **Empathy** — The novel's ethical core: understanding others across difference.\n- 🏘️ **Class & Social Hierarchy** — Maycomb's caste system (Finches, Cunninghams, Ewells, Black community) is carefully mapped.\n- 🦅 **The \"Mockingbird\" Symbol** — Innocents (Tom Robinson, Boo Radley) destroyed or endangered by a cruel society.\n\n---\n\n## 3 & 4. ⚠️ A Note on the Passages (Requests 3 & 4)\n\nI must be straightforward with you: **the excerpt tool did not return the correct text**. Both calls returned an identical passage that clearly does not belong to *To Kill a Mockingbird* (it mentions characters named \"Joshua,\" \"Arnold,\" \"Mr. Ridley,\" and \"Sherman Jackson\" — none of whom appear in Lee's novel). Presenting that text to you as Scout's opening or Tom's testimony would be academically harmful, especially for a close-reading exercise.\n\n**What I recommend for retrieving the actual passages:**\n- **Your licensed copy** (physical or digital) is your most reliable source — Chapter 1 opens with Scout's famous reflection on Jem's broken arm and the Finch family history; Tom's testimony is in **Chapter 19**.\n- If you're working digitally, the **HarperCollins e-book** or your institution's **library database (e.g., EBSCO, ProQuest, or your LMS)** will have the exact text.\n- For quick reference, **SparkNotes' quote database** and the **Purdue OWL's literary resources** often have key passages cleared for educational use.\n\nI'd rather send you to your licensed copy with accurate chapter numbers than give you a wrong passage for a close-reading exercise. Would you like me to help you build the analysis framework or discussion questions for those two passages instead?",
